 ## Overview
-Half-angle slicing is a two-pass per-slice rendering technique for computing volumetric shadows [S1, unofficial][S5, book]. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation through a volume without pre-computing or storing large 3D shadow maps [S2][S5, book]. By orienting slice planes perpendicular to the half-angle vector between the eye and light directions, a single slice geometry can be rendered from both viewpoints, allowing the same rasterized primitives to compute eye-composited color and light transmissivity [S1, unofficial][S5, book]. It differs from standard view-aligned slicing by minimizing the number of slices required to resolve lighting details and from light-aligned slicing by maintaining visibility coherence for the observer [S4, unofficial].
+Half-angle slicing is a two-pass per-slice rendering technique for computing volumetric shadows [S2, unofficial][S6, book]. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation through a volume without pre-computing or storing large 3D shadow maps [S3][S6, book]. By orienting slice planes perpendicular to the half-angle vector between the eye and light directions, a single slice geometry can be rendered from both viewpoints, allowing the same rasterized primitives to compute eye-composited color and light transmissivity [S2, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. It differs from standard view-aligned slicing by minimizing the number of slices required to resolve lighting details and from light-aligned slicing by maintaining visibility coherence for the observer [S4, unofficial].
 
